---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Walking Pad (2026): 6 Picks and Which One to Buy";
const description =
  "What is the best walking pad? Our pick for most people, plus budget, long-belt, heavy-duty, incline and jogging picks, each checked against two sources.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "walkingpad-a1-pro",
    name: "WalkingPad A1 Pro",
    category: "Best Overall Walking Pad",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=WalkingPad+A1+Pro+Foldable+Under+Desk+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$529–$699",
    priceBounds: {"low":529,"high":699},
    specs: "47 x 16.5 in. walking area · 0.5–3.75 mph · 300 lb capacity · 63 lb · folds in half",
    pros: [
      "A 47-inch walking area, long enough for a natural stride at a brisk walk and for most taller walkers",
      "Rated for 300 lb, higher than most folding pads, on both WalkingPad’s page and independent coverage",
      "Folds in half and arrives fully assembled, so it stores under a bed or sofa",
      "Speed from the remote, the app or FootSense, which follows your position on the belt",
    ],
    cons: [
      "WalkingPad and an independent tracker give very different motor ratings, so we have not printed one",
      "At about $529 on sale it costs well over twice the budget pick, and the warranty is one year",
      "Tops out at 3.75 mph and has no incline, so it will not make your walk harder as you get fitter",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The walking pad to buy if you want one machine that suits most people and most rooms: a long belt, a generous weight limit and a frame that folds away.",
    description: "The A1 Pro is WalkingPad's long-running folding pad, and it earns the top spot by doing the things that matter most without a serious weakness. WalkingPad lists a 47 by 16.5-inch walking area, a speed range of 0.5 to 3.75 mph, a 300 lb maximum user weight and a 63 lb net weight, and an independent spec tracker gives the same walking area, top speed, capacity and weight. That belt is among the longest on any pad, so a normal stride fits at a brisk walk, and the 300 lb rating leaves real margin for most walkers. It folds in half to about 5 inches tall by WalkingPad's figures, and an independent reviewer who has used one in an office for a long time found it fairly quiet at desk speeds. Two things to know. WalkingPad's specification table still mentions 230 lb and 300 lb versions, though only the 300 lb version was for sale on its store when we checked; and the motor is listed by WalkingPad at 3.75 HP while the tracker gives a much lower figure, so we left horsepower out. WalkingPad listed it at $529, down from $699, on 18 October 2026, with a one-year warranty.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-spacewalk-e1l",
    name: "UREVO SpaceWalk E1L",
    category: "Best Budget Walking Pad",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+SpaceWalk+E1L+Walking+Pad&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$140–$190",
    priceBounds: {"low":140,"high":190},
    specs: "35.4 x 15 in. belt · up to 4.0 mph · 36.6 lb · flat deck, no incline",
    pros: [
      "Often sold for well under $200 — UREVO showed $139.99 against $189.99 when we checked",
      "At about 37 lb, the lightest pad here and the easiest to slide under furniture every day",
      "Belt size, top speed and unit weight agree between UREVO and an independent spec tracker",
      "App connection and a remote, with a warranty extension on registration",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 35.4-inch belt is short; it suits slow desk walking, not a brisk pace or a long stride",
      "UREVO rates it for 265 lb and the tracker for 242 lb, so we have not printed a capacity",
      "The tracker reports a brushed motor well below the advertised 2.5 HP, and there is no incline",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest pad we would buy for slow walking at a desk or around the house. If you are tall, walk briskly or weigh over about 220 lb, spend more.",
    description: "The SpaceWalk E1L is UREVO's entry-level flat pad and the budget pick here because its basic figures held up, which is rarer than it should be at this price. UREVO lists a 35.43 by 15-inch running area, a speed range of 0.6 to 4.0 mph and a 36.59 lb unit weight, and an independent spec tracker gives the same belt, top speed and weight. That makes it the lightest pad on this page and the easiest to move every day. The figures that differ are capacity — 265 lb on UREVO's page, 242 lb on the tracker's — and deck height, so neither appears on the card; if you are near either weight figure, choose a pad with more margin. The tracker also reports a brushed motor and a rated power well below the advertised 2.5 HP, which is normal for a budget pad and a reason to keep sessions to walking pace. The belt is the real limit: at about 35 inches it is fine for slow desk walking, but taller walkers and anyone walking briskly will want the Z1. UREVO's warranty is one year plus 180 days, with the extension depending on registration in its app. UREVO showed $139.99, down from $189.99, on 18 October 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "walkingpad-z1",
    name: "WalkingPad Z1",
    category: "Folding Pad With the Longest Belt for the Money",
    badge: "Longest Belt",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H2YYZTL8?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: "/walkingpad-z1-review/",
    priceRange: "$299–$329",
    priceBounds: {"low":299,"high":329},
    specs: "47.6 in. walking area length · 1–4 mph · 242 lb capacity · about 51 lb · folds in half",
    pros: [
      "The longest walking area on this page, 47.6 inches, for about $300",
      "WalkingPad, a second retailer and an independent tracker agree on its length, speed and capacity",
      "Folds in half, arrives assembled and stores in a small footprint",
      "A walking-only speed range, which suits desks and shared floors",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 242 lb limit, lower than the A1 Pro’s, leaves little margin for heavier walkers",
      "Motor ratings differ widely between WalkingPad and the other sources",
      "No incline and no handlebar",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pad to buy if you want a long belt that folds away and you are comfortably under 242 lb. It is most of the A1 Pro for a few hundred dollars less.",
    description: "The Z1 is the value alternative to our top pick: nearly the same belt, a lower weight limit and a much lower price. WalkingPad lists a 47.6 by 15.7-inch walking area, a speed range of 1 to 4 mph, a 242 lb maximum user weight and a 51 lb net weight, folding in half; Living.fit, which also sells it, gives the same length, speed range and unit weight and a 110 kg capacity, and an independent spec tracker agrees on the 242 lb limit and 4 mph top speed. The width differs by a tenth of an inch between listings, so we printed the length only. The motor is where sources part company — WalkingPad says 2.25 HP brushless, Living.fit and the tracker 0.75 HP — and the noise claims conflict too: WalkingPad's Z1 page says below 65 dB while Living.fit's says below 40 dB, which shows how little such figures can be compared. On 18 October 2026 WalkingPad had the standard Z1 in stock at $329 and the white Z1 at $299. The warranty is one year.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-novawalk-w50-trekpad",
    name: "MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad",
    category: "Best Walking Pad for Heavier Users",
    badge: "Best for Heavier Users",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HJTT2S6R?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$300–$480",
    priceBounds: {"low":300,"high":480},
    specs: "400 lb capacity · 41.3 x 16.5 in. belt · up to 4.0 mph · 1.25 HP continuous motor · powered incline",
    pros: [
      "A 400 lb rating stated by MERACH, the Amazon listing title and independent coverage",
      "MERACH publishes a continuous motor rating, 1.25 HP, alongside the 3.5 HP peak — few pad makers do",
      "A 16.5-inch-wide belt, wider than most pads, with a powered incline",
      "Sold well below list price — $299.99 against $479.99 when we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not fold in half; it stands upright for storage, and at about 69 lb it is heavy to move",
      "MERACH’s page calls the incline both 12% and 12 degrees, and one Amazon field gives a 150 kg capacity",
      "The base warranty is one year, with a second year only after registering with MERACH",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pad to buy if you are a heavier walker and want a capacity figure that holds up across sources. Leave a margin under the 400 lb rating.",
    description: "The W50 TrekPad is our pick for heavier walkers because its weight rating is unusually consistent and its motor figure unusually honest. MERACH's product page lists a 400 lb maximum user weight, a 16.5 by 41.3-inch belt and a motor rated at 1.25 HP continuous and 3.5 HP peak, and an independent spec tracker gives the same capacity, belt, continuous rating and a 4 mph top speed. A continuous rating is the figure that tells you how a motor copes with long sessions under load, and most pad makers publish only the peak. The incline is powered; MERACH's page title, the Amazon listing and the tracker give 12%, though one line of MERACH's description says 12 degrees, which would be far steeper, so treat 12% as the working figure. Two smaller cautions: one technical field on the Amazon listing gives 150 kg rather than 400 lb, and a cheaper W50 Lite with a lower rating is sold alongside it, so check the model number (MERACH lists MR-T25B2-US). The warranty is one year, with a second on registration. MERACH listed it at $299.99, down from $479.99, on 18 October 2026. Our heavy-people roundup covers four more high-capacity pads.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-spacewalk-3s",
    name: "UREVO SpaceWalk 3S",
    category: "Best Walking Pad With Incline",
    badge: "Best With Incline",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+SpaceWalk+3S+Walking+Pad&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$250–$350",
    priceBounds: {"low":250,"high":350},
    specs: "0–9% powered incline · 0.6–4.0 mph · 42.5 in. belt length · 265 lb capacity · 6.5 in. tall",
    pros: [
      "Powered incline from 0 to 9%, changed mid-walk from the remote or the UREVO app",
      "A 42.5-inch belt, long for an incline pad, which matters because your stride changes uphill",
      "Incline, speed, belt, capacity and height agree between UREVO and an independent tracker",
      "Strong value — UREVO showed $249.99 against $349.99 when we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Unit weight is about 57 lb on UREVO’s page and about 71 lb on the tracker’s, so plan on the heavier figure",
      "The tracker reports a brushed motor rated well below the advertised 2.5 HP maximum",
      "At 6.5 inches tall it sits higher than a flat pad, and UREVO now sells a newer SpaceWalk 5L at a higher price",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest way to get a genuine, adjustable incline on a belt long enough to use it. The right pad if you want your walk to get harder as you get fitter.",
    description: "The SpaceWalk 3S is the pad to choose if incline is what you want, and it costs about half the price of our top pick. UREVO lists a powered incline from 0 to 9% across nine levels, a speed range of 0.6 to 4.0 mph, a 42.5 by 16.54-inch running area, a 265 lb maximum load and a 6.5-inch height, and an independent spec tracker gives the same incline, speed, belt length, capacity and height. Incline is the only way to raise the effort on a pad once 4 mph feels easy, and even a few percent at a desk pace is a noticeably harder walk; the longer-than-usual belt keeps that comfortable. The disagreements are on weight — 56.65 lb on UREVO's page, 71 lb on the tracker's — and the motor, which UREVO advertises at 2.5 HP maximum and the tracker, citing the manual, rates much lower and describes as brushed. The warranty is one year plus 180 days, with the extension requiring registration in UREVO's app. The tracker notes UREVO's SpaceWalk 5L as a newer model; the 3S was still on sale at $249.99, down from $349.99, on UREVO's store on 18 October 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-strol-2s-pro",
    name: "UREVO Strol 2S Pro",
    category: "Best Walking Pad for Light Jogging",
    badge: "Best for Jogging",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+Strol+2S+Pro+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$300–$550",
    priceBounds: {"low":300,"high":550},
    specs: "42.5 x 16.5 in. belt · to 4.0 mph bar down, 7.6 mph bar up · 0–9% powered incline · 265 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Raising the handlebar unlocks a running mode to 7.6 mph, enough for an easy jog for many people",
      "Walking mode reaches 4.0 mph with the bar down, so it still works under a desk",
      "Powered incline to 9% as well, which few 2-in-1 pads offer",
      "Belt, speeds, incline and capacity agree between UREVO and an independent tracker",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 42.5-inch belt is long for a pad but short for running; treat jogging as an occasional extra",
      "About 75 lb, the heaviest pad here, and the tracker reports a brushed motor below the advertised 2.5 HP",
      "The price swings widely — $549.99 on UREVO’s store when we checked, against about $300 on sale days earlier",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pad to buy if you want a handlebar and the option of a short, easy jog alongside desk walking. If you want to run regularly, buy a treadmill instead.",
    description: "The Strol 2S Pro is a 2-in-1: a walking pad with a handlebar that folds up, and with the bar raised, a higher speed range. UREVO lists a 42.52 by 16.54-inch running area, a walking mode of 0.6 to 4.0 mph, a running mode to 7.6 mph, a 0 to 9% powered incline, a 265 lb maximum load and a 74.51 lb unit weight, and an independent spec tracker agrees on the belt, both speed limits, the incline, the capacity and a weight of about 75 lb. That makes it the most capable pad here for light jogging, with the important caveat that a 42.5-inch belt is far shorter than a running treadmill's, so a jog suits a compact, easy stride rather than real running. Walking mode still reaches 4 mph with the bar folded, so it works under a desk too. The tracker describes the motor as brushed and rated well below UREVO's 2.5 HP maximum, so do not plan long, fast sessions. The warranty is one year plus 180 days on app registration. The price moves a lot: UREVO's store showed $549.99 on 18 October 2026, and it was on sale near $300 earlier in the month, so it is worth waiting for a discount.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Walking Pads", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-pads/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people, the WalkingPad A1 Pro: it has a 47-inch walking area that fits a normal stride, a 300 lb weight rating and a frame that folds in half. If you want to spend under $200, the UREVO SpaceWalk E1L is our budget pick; for incline, the UREVO SpaceWalk 3S; and for heavier walkers, the MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best walking pad to buy on a budget?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The UREVO SpaceWalk E1L, which UREVO was selling for $139.99 when we checked. Its belt size, top speed and weight agree between UREVO and independent coverage. Its 35.4-inch belt suits slow desk walking rather than a brisk pace, so taller walkers and brisk walkers should look at the WalkingPad Z1 at about $300.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I look for in a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Belt length against your height and pace first, then the weight limit with a margin to spare, then whether it folds or has incline. Treat peak horsepower and decibel figures with caution, because listings disagree on them most. Check the deck height against your standing desk, the warranty terms and the return window before you order.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad the same as a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a kind of treadmill, built smaller: a low deck, no tall console, a top speed around 4 mph and usually a belt of 35 to 48 inches. A full treadmill has a longer belt, a stronger motor, handrails and higher speeds, and it suits running. A walking pad suits walking at a desk or at home and stores away more easily.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you jog on a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not on most flat pads, which stop at about 3.75 to 4 mph. A 2-in-1 pad such as the UREVO Strol 2S Pro unlocks a higher speed when the handlebar is raised, which allows a light, easy jog. Its belt is still far shorter than a running treadmill's, so treat jogging as an occasional extra and follow the manual's limits.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do walking pads last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on use, weight and maintenance more than on the brand. Under daily desk use, a budget pad commonly lasts a few years rather than a decade. Staying well under the weight limit, lubricating and centring the belt as the manual says, and keeping to walking speeds all help. Warranties on pads are usually one year, sometimes extended on registration.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-18",
      dateModified: "2026-10-18",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-pads/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-18",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-18",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best walking pad",
      "what is the best walking pad",
      "best walking pad to buy",
      "best walking pad treadmill",
      "walking pad",
      "under desk treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Best Walking Pads", link: "#quick-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestwalkingpads" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestwalkingpads)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          What Is the Best <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Walking Pad?</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          For most people it is a folding pad with a belt long enough for a natural stride and a weight limit with room to spare. Here are six walking pads worth buying in 2026, one for each of the jobs people actually buy them for, and the figures that held up when we checked them against the maker and an independent source.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#which-type" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Which Type Do You Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 18, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          walking pads. Each was confirmed on 18 October 2026 as currently sold on its maker's own
          US store (WalkingPad, UREVO and MERACH), and its specifications were checked on that date
          against the maker's product page and at least one independent source — a long-running
          spec tracker that reads owner's manuals and motor labels, or a second retailer. A figure
          appears in a product's specification line only where those sources agree; where they did
          not — most often on motor ratings, weight limits, unit weight and deck height — we left
          it off the card and say so in the text. We searched the US Consumer Product Safety
          Commission's recall database for every brand on this page and found no recalls; Sperax
          pads are excluded after the CPSC's April 2026 warning. None of the figures are our own
          measurements. Prices are those the makers showed on 18 October 2026 and change often, as
          do specifications under unchanged product names, so confirm the price, specification and
          warranty terms against the exact listing before you order. We earn a commission if you
          buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list or
          what we say about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Overall:</span>
            <a href="#walkingpad-a1-pro" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">WalkingPad A1 Pro</a>
            <a href="#walkingpad-a1-pro" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Budget:</span>
            <a href="#urevo-spacewalk-e1l" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">UREVO SpaceWalk E1L</a>
            <a href="#urevo-spacewalk-e1l" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
